    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: India's total fertility rate has fallen below replacement level (around 2.0), and its median age, while still young relative to China and developed economies, is rising -- meaning India's demographic dividend window (a high working-age-to-dependent ratio) is time-limited and requires active policy conversion into economic dividend before it closes.\n\nWhy conversion isn't automatic: A large working-age population only becomes an economic dividend if it is healthy, skilled, and productively employed; without adequate human-capital investment and job creation, a large youth cohort can instead become a demographic burden (unemployment, social instability) rather than a dividend -- as seen in the current mismatch between rising labour-force entrants and formal-sector job creation.\n\nPolicy measures for conversion: (i) Education and skilling -- substantially improving school learning outcomes, expanding vocational training and skill-certification (Skill India Mission) aligned to actual industry/labour-market demand, and increasing higher-education access and quality. (ii) Health investment -- ensuring the workforce is physically and mentally productive through improved public healthcare, nutrition (addressing persistent stunting/anaemia), and maternal-child health, since human capital quality determines productivity, not headcount alone. (iii) Employment generation -- accelerating labour-intensive manufacturing growth (Production-Linked Incentive schemes, 'Make in India'), formalising the informal sector to extend social security and productivity gains, and removing labour-market rigidities that discourage formal hiring. (iv) Female labour-force participation -- India's female workforce participation remains low by international standards; raising it (through childcare support, safety, and workplace-inclusion policy) would substantially expand the effective working-age contributing population. (v) Regional targeting -- since fertility decline and ageing are occurring unevenly (southern/western states ageing faster than northern/eastern states), a two-track strategy is needed: leveraging still-young northern/eastern state populations for labour supply while addressing ageing-related productivity and social-security needs in the south/west. (vi) Urbanisation and infrastructure -- ensuring cities can absorb rural-to-urban labour migration productively, with adequate housing, transport and services.\n\nConclusion: India's dividend window is finite -- UN projections suggest it narrows from around the 2040s-50s as the population itself ages -- making the coming two decades a critical, time-bound opportunity that requires urgent, coordinated investment in health, education, skilling and job creation rather than passive reliance on demographic structure alone.",
