    wordLimit: 250,
    questionText: "Highlight the salient features of India's trade policy. Discuss the status of India's balance of trade with China.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Salient features of India's trade policy: India's Foreign Trade Policy framework (most recently the FTP 2023, a shift to an open-ended, dynamically updated policy rather than fixed five-year cycles) emphasises: (i) export promotion through schemes like RoDTEP (Remission of Duties and Taxes on Exported Products) and interest-equalisation support, replacing earlier WTO-inconsistent schemes; (ii) ease of doing business via digitisation of trade documentation and paperless processing; (iii) promotion of districts as export hubs to broaden export participation beyond traditional industrial clusters; (iv) special focus on e-commerce exports and dual-use/strategic-goods export facilitation; (v) a strategic pivot toward Free Trade Agreements with select partners (UAE, Australia, and ongoing negotiations with the EU and UK) rather than broad multilateral liberalisation, reflecting a calibrated, interest-based trade diplomacy; and (vi) import policy calibration -- tariff protection for select domestic manufacturing sectors (aligned with 'Atmanirbhar Bharat'/PLI schemes) alongside targeted liberalisation for inputs needed by export industries.\n\nBalance of trade with China: India runs a large and persistent trade deficit with China, its largest bilateral merchandise trade deficit with any country, driven by heavy dependence on Chinese imports of electronics/telecom equipment, machinery, active pharmaceutical ingredient intermediates, and industrial inputs, while Indian exports to China (ores, cotton, organic chemicals) remain comparatively limited and volatile. This deficit has widened over the past decade despite periodic bilateral political tensions (post-Galwan border clashes), reflecting India's continued structural dependence on Chinese manufacturing inputs even as diplomatic relations remain strained -- a tension the government has sought to address through import-substitution measures (PLI schemes in electronics, APIs, solar equipment) and scrutiny of Chinese investment, though near-term structural dependence remains significant given the depth of existing supply-chain integration.",
    examinerNotes:
      "The China trade-deficit discussion should note the apparent paradox of a large and growing deficit persisting despite political/border tensions -- this tension is exactly what distinguishes a sophisticated answer from a purely descriptive statistic-recitation one. Mentioning specific policy instruments (RoDTEP, FTP 2023, PLI) for the first part avoids a vague, unsupported description of 'trade policy.'",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_A("4(c)"),
