    questionText: "Identify the jute-producing areas in India and discuss the major causes of decline of jute mill industry in India.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Jute cultivation in India is concentrated overwhelmingly in the Ganga-Brahmaputra deltaic tract, where high humidity, heavy rainfall (150-250 cm), high temperature, and fertile alluvial soil with good water retention provide ideal conditions. West Bengal is by far the largest producer (deltaic districts such as Nadia, Murshidabad, Malda, North & South 24 Parganas), followed by Bihar, Assam, and Odisha, with the Hooghly riverbanks historically forming the core of the associated jute-milling belt (Kolkata/Howrah region).\n\nCauses of decline of the jute mill industry: Competition from cheaper synthetic substitutes (polypropylene/plastic packaging) has steadily eroded jute's traditional packaging-material market. Ageing, technologically outdated mills (many dating to the colonial era) have failed to modernise, raising costs and reducing competitiveness against synthetic alternatives and other jute-exporting nations (notably Bangladesh). Labour unrest, frequent mill closures/lockouts, and financial stress among jute companies have caused chronic underutilisation of capacity. Shrinking export demand, as global buyers shifted to synthetic packaging, further weakened the industry, despite government support measures such as the Jute Packaging Materials Act mandating jute use for foodgrain/sugar packaging, which has only partially offset the structural decline.",
