    questionText: "Explain the geological characteristics and ecological significance of the Eastern Ghats.",
    modelAnswer:
      "The Eastern Ghats are a discontinuous, highly eroded range of ancient Precambrian metamorphic rocks (khondalites, charnockites, gneisses) running roughly parallel to India's east coast from Odisha to Tamil Nadu, geologically far older and more heavily denuded than the younger, more continuous Western Ghats -- their broken, discontinuous character results from prolonged erosion by east-flowing peninsular rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) which have cut wide gaps through the range on their way to the Bay of Bengal, unlike the Western Ghats' continuous escarpment.\n\nEcological significance: Despite lower average elevation and rainfall than the Western Ghats, the Eastern Ghats harbour significant dry and moist deciduous forest biodiversity, including several wildlife sanctuaries and tiger reserves (e.g., in the Eastern Ghats' Andhra Pradesh-Odisha stretches). They form an important watershed for peninsular rivers feeding the Bay of Bengal, support tribal communities dependent on forest resources, and their mineral-rich rock base (bauxite, iron ore) makes them ecologically sensitive zones where mining pressure often conflicts directly with forest and tribal-livelihood conservation.",
