    modelAnswer:
      "India's cultural regionalisation, following geographers like O.H.K. Spate and later regional-culture scholars, is typically based on attributes such as language, religion, dominant social customs, dress, cuisine, architecture, and historical-political heritage rather than purely physical boundaries.\n\nBroad cultural regions include: North India (Indo-Gangetic Hindi-Urdu belt, wheat-based cuisine, historically shaped by successive Indo-Aryan and Indo-Islamic influences); Northeast India (culturally and linguistically distinct Tibeto-Burman-influenced tribal societies with strong community/clan-based social organisation, distinct from mainland Hindu-caste social structure); East India (Bengali/Odia cultural zone, strong literary-artistic tradition, rice-fish cuisine); West India (Gujarati-Marathi commercial/mercantile cultural tradition, Jain-Hindu religious mix); Central India (tribal-dominated cultural zone, e.g., Gond and other Adivasi traditions); South India (Dravidian-language cultural zone -- Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam -- distinct temple-architecture tradition, rice-based cuisine, historically less influenced by Indo-Islamic rule than the north); and the Himalayan cultural zone (Buddhist-influenced, high-altitude pastoral/agrarian communities in Ladakh, Sikkim, and parts of Himachal/Uttarakhand). These regions are not rigidly bounded but represent gradational cultural attribute clusters shaped by historical migration, religion, and physical-environmental adaptation.",
