    section: "B",
    questionNumber: "6(a)",
    marks: 20,
    wordLimit: 350,
    questionText: "A large number of Indian cities have complex morphological characteristics due to their historical evolution. Elucidate.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: Urban morphology -- the physical form, street pattern, land-use arrangement and building fabric of a city -- in India frequently reflects layered historical development rather than planned, uniform design, producing complex, often contradictory spatial patterns within a single city.\n\nPre-colonial cores: Many Indian cities retain a historic core with organic, narrow, winding street patterns clustered around a religious, royal or trading nucleus (e.g., the walled city of Old Delhi around the Red Fort/Jama Masjid, the old city of Jaipur with its distinctive planned grid reflecting Vastu-based Rajput town planning, or Varanasi's dense riverside ghats-oriented core) -- these zones typically show high building density, mixed residential-commercial use, and street patterns unsuited to modern vehicular traffic.\n\nColonial-era additions: British-era 'civil lines' and cantonment areas were deliberately planned with wide, tree-lined avenues, low-density bungalow housing, and segregated administrative/military zones (e.g., New Delhi's Lutyens' zone, Bengaluru's Cantonment area, Pune's Camp area) -- producing a sharp morphological contrast with the adjoining older 'native town' areas, a duality still visible in many Indian cities today.\n\nPost-independence planned extensions: Government-planned townships and satellite developments (Chandigarh's Corbusier-designed sectoral grid, Bhubaneswar's early planned layout, and various state capital/industrial townships) introduced a third morphological layer -- rationalised zoning, wide roads, and functional segregation, contrasting sharply with both the pre-colonial core and colonial cantonment patterns.\n\nPost-liberalisation/contemporary sprawl: Since the 1990s, rapid economic growth has driven unplanned peripheral expansion -- IT-corridor development (Bengaluru's Whitefield/Electronics City, Gurugram's private-sector-led high-rise/commercial sprawl), often growing faster than formal planning and infrastructure can absorb, producing a fourth, frequently poorly integrated morphological layer.\n\nResulting complexity: A single large Indian city (Delhi is the clearest example) can therefore contain, within a short distance, a dense pre-colonial walled core, colonial-era low-density civil lines, planned post-independence government colonies, and unplanned contemporary peripheral sprawl -- each with distinct street patterns, building density, and infrastructure standards, making integrated city-wide planning, transport design and service delivery exceptionally difficult, and explaining much of the fragmented governance and infrastructure-mismatch problems seen in Indian urban management today.\n\nConclusion: Indian urban morphology is thus best read as a historical palimpsest -- successive eras of development overlaid rather than replaced -- and understanding this layered evolution is essential to designing planning interventions appropriate to each zone's distinct morphological character.",
    examinerNotes:
      "The strongest answers use the 'palimpsest'/layered-history framing explicitly and name concrete examples for each historical layer (pre-colonial core, colonial cantonment, post-independence planned town, contemporary sprawl) rather than discussing morphology in the abstract. Delhi is the most efficient single-city example to illustrate all four layers at once.",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_B("6(a)"),
