    marks: 15,
    wordLimit: 250,
    questionText: "Discuss the significance of organic farming for sustainable agricultural development in India.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Organic farming -- cultivation without synthetic fertilisers, pesticides or GM inputs, relying instead on biological inputs (compost, biofertilisers, crop rotation, biological pest control) -- holds significance for sustainable agricultural development in India along several dimensions.\n\nEnvironmental significance: Organic practices reduce soil degradation and improve long-term soil health (organic matter content, microbial activity, water-holding capacity), reversing damage from decades of intensive chemical-input Green Revolution agriculture in regions like Punjab-Haryana where soil health and groundwater have visibly deteriorated. It reduces agro-chemical runoff into water bodies (mitigating eutrophication) and lowers the carbon/energy footprint associated with synthetic fertiliser production.\n\nEconomic significance: Organic produce commands premium prices in domestic and export markets (India is a leading global exporter of organic products, notably from Madhya Pradesh, Sikkim -- India's first fully organic state -- and parts of the Northeast), offering smallholder farmers a potential value-addition pathway, though certification costs and yield transition risk (often lower yields in early conversion years) remain real constraints.\n\nSocial/health significance: Reduced pesticide residue benefits both farmer health (lower chemical exposure) and consumer health, addressing rising food-safety concerns; organic farming's typically higher labour intensity can also support rural employment.\n\nSustainability trade-offs: Organic farming alone cannot easily match Green Revolution-era yield levels needed for national food security at current population scale, meaning it is best positioned as a complementary strategy -- suited to specific regions, crops (horticulture, spices, tea) and farmer segments -- alongside broader sustainable-intensification approaches (natural farming, integrated nutrient management) rather than a wholesale replacement for conventional agriculture nationwide. India's Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana and Sikkim's full-state organic conversion are instructive policy models for scaling organic adoption where locally appropriate.",
