    wordLimit: 250,
    questionText: "Examine the geopolitical impact of bilateral relationship between India and Sri Lanka.",
    modelAnswer:
      "India-Sri Lanka relations are shaped by deep geographic proximity (separated only by the Palk Strait), historical-cultural-religious ties (Buddhism, Tamil ethnic linkages), and India's core strategic interest in preventing hostile external power presence in an island located directly astride major Indian Ocean shipping lanes just off India's southern coast.\n\nEconomic dimension: India is among Sri Lanka's largest trading partners and a major source of investment and development assistance; India's decisive support during Sri Lanka's 2022 economic/debt crisis (extending several billion dollars in credit lines, fuel, food and currency-swap support) significantly reinforced bilateral goodwill and demonstrated India's role as first-responder neighbour ('Neighbourhood First' policy in action).\n\nStrategic/geopolitical dimension: India's central concern is countering the expanding footprint of China in Sri Lanka -- notably Chinese-funded infrastructure projects (Hambantota Port, taken on a 99-year lease by China after Sri Lanka's debt distress, and Colombo Port City) which India views as strategically significant 'String of Pearls'-type developments potentially enabling Chinese naval access near Indian waters. India has responded by expanding its own connectivity, energy and port-development cooperation (e.g., involvement in Trincomalee-related energy infrastructure and the Colombo/Kankesanthurai port projects) to balance Chinese influence through its own economic engagement rather than confrontation.\n\nEthnic/domestic dimension: The Tamil ethnic question in Sri Lanka (relating to the Sri Lankan Tamil minority, post-civil-war reconciliation, and implementation of the 13th Amendment's devolution provisions) remains a sensitive factor in bilateral relations, given domestic political pressure from Tamil Nadu on the Indian government to advocate for Tamil rights and devolution.\n\nOverall, the relationship illustrates how India balances economic-humanitarian goodwill, strategic competition with China, and domestic ethnic-politics sensitivity within a single, geographically proximate bilateral relationship.",
